-The human visual system is a pattern-matching engine optimized for the periodicities of the natural world: leaves, branches, ripples, stone fractures. When confronted with a nine-fold symmetry, the visual cortex cannot resolve it into a recognizable lattice. It enters a productive failure mode — the pattern-matching loop continues running without converging, producing sustained attention without resolution. The brain stays in the loop because the loop has no exit condition. The Sri Yantra is adversarial art for the visual cortex. It does not ask for devotion. It asks for processing cycles.
+Following the nine triangles, my eye keeps asking where the lines will meet. Adversarial art is the comparison I reach for: a drawing that resists being put away after one glance. That difficulty gives me an entry into attention, without turning the triangle count into a claim about cortical failure.
 
